   "id": "ca_banner_peak_northeast_buttress",
   "area_id": "ca_banner_peak",
   "name": "Northeast Buttress",
   "grade": "5.6",
   "discipline": "alpine",
   "overview": "Descent is via the Banner-Ritter notch, then a glissade to Lake Catherine. Decent stone on the technical parts. Reach the shoulder under the route over snow, starting from the Gem Lakes Trailhead past Thousand Island Lakes. The hardest moves are early, angling up and right. Higher, a corner system beside a big buttress gives way to loose, easy scrambling and then ledges leading to a wide face; go around a corner to the right as it steepens. Mostly 4th class.",
   "description": null,
   "approach": "The approach from the Gem Lakes trailhead runs to the Thousand Island Lakes basin, which makes a good camp (mosquitoes can be thick in early summer). From camp, ascend the snowfield up to the shoulder under the climb and transition to the rock there; an ice axe is needed on the snow, and crampons if it is frozen hard.",
   "descent_text": "Head down toward the saddle separating Banner from Ritter, where both sides hold snow, so carry an ice axe. From the saddle, glissade the snow slope below it back toward the lakes.",
